   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Bom dia! Bem vindo ao Brasil! &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Welcome to our Cachaca brewing station! &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Cachaca is made from pure sugar cane. First, it's harvested from May to December. Because Brazil is a tropical climate, sugar cane can grow in almost every single state. After harvesting, the sugarcane is cleaned. and the juice is extracted. From there it's fermented using yeast for about 14-28 hours. Distillation occurs in copper pot stills which increases the alcohol content. It can then be aged in stainless steel or wood barrels depending on the desired flavor. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Cachaca was originally produced by slaves in sugarcane mills during the 1500s. Becuase it became so popular among peasants, the Portuguese crown tried to ban it, which led to a revolt in 1660. Eventually, Cachaca became a symbol of national pride and revolution in the lower classes. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Today, Cachaca is one of the most popular alcohols in Brazil. It's most popularly used to make Caipirinha, another Brazilian cocktail. While it has gained more recoginition internationally in recent years, it still is a domestic product. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
